 Pair | Slope | Sunny? |\n|------|-------|-------|\n| \\(P_1P_2\\) | undefined (vertical) | No |\n| \\(P_1P_3\\) | undefined | No |\n| \\(P_1P_4\\) | \\(0\\) (horizontal) | No |\n| \\(P_1P_5\\) | \\(1\\) | **Yes** |\n| \\(P_1P_6\\) | \\(0\\) | No |\n| \\(P_2P_3\\) | undefined | No |\n| \\(P_2P_4\\) | \\(-1\\) | No |\n| \\(P_2P_5\\) | \\(0\\) | No |\n| \\(P_2P_6\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) | **Yes** |\n| \\(P_3P_4\\) | \\(-2\\) | **Yes** |\n| \\(P_3P_5\\) | \\(-1\\) | No |\n| \\(P_3P_6\\) | \\(-1\\) | No |\n| \\(P_4P_5\\) | undefined | No |\n| \\(P_4P_6\\) | \\(0\\) | No |\n| \\(P_5P_6\\) | \\(-1\\) | No |\n\nThus the **only** sunny lines that pass through two points of \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}: P_{1}P_{5}\\;(1,1)-(2,2),\\qquad\n\\ell_{b}: P_{2}P_{6}\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{c}: P_{3}P_{4}\\;(1,3)-(2,1).\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly the two points indicated; no sunny line can contain three points of \\(S\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny lines through points of \\(S\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nChecking all such lines that intersect \\(S\\) we obtain:\n\n* Vertical lines: \\(x=1\\) (contains \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\)), \\(x=2\\) (contains \\(P_{4},P_{5}\\)), \\(x=3\\) (contains \\(P_{6}\\)).\n* Horizontal lines: \\(y=1\\) (contains \\(P_{1},P_{4},P_{6}\\)), \\(y=2\\) (contains \\(P_{2},P_{5}\\)), \\(y=3\\) (contains \\(P_{3}\\)).\n* Slope \\(-1\\) lines: \\(x+y=2\\) (contains \\(P_{1}\\)), \\(x+y=3\\) (contains \\(P_{2},P_{4}\\)), \\(x+y=4\\) (contains \\(P_{3},P_{5},P_{6}\\)).\n\nHence any non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(S\\); the only line attaining three points is \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) cover all points of \\(S\\), exactly two of them are sunny, and the third is non‑sunny.\n\nBecause a sunny line can contain at most two