tal) | no |\n| \\(BD, CE, CF, EF\\) | \\(-1\\) | no |\n| \\(AE\\) | \\(1\\) | **yes** |\n| \\(BF\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) | **yes** |\n| \\(CD\\) | \\(-2\\) | **yes** |\n\nAll other pairs have slope \\(0\\), undefined, or \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently **any** sunny line that contains two points of \\(P\\) must be one of\n\n\\[\nL_{AE}: y=x,\\qquad L_{BF}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad L_{CD}: y=-2x+5 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNo other sunny line can pass through any point of \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake three non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_3 &: x+y=4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n- \\(L_1\\) (vertical) contains \\(A,B,C\\).  \n- \\(L_2\\) (horizontal) contains \\(A,D,F\\).  \n- \\(L_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) contains \\(C,E,F\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(P\\) lies on at least one of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nAll three lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nChoose the sunny line \\(L_{AE}\\) from (1) and add two non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x \\quad(\\text{sunny}),\\\\\nL_2 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_3 &: y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nVerification:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\nA(1,1) & L_1\\\\\nE(2,2) & L_1\\\\\nB(1,2) & L_2\\\\\nC(1,3) & L_2\\\\\nD(2,1) & L_3\\\\\nF(3,1) & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nExactly one line (\\(L_1\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\).\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nTake the three sunny lines listed in (1):\n\n\\[\nL_1:y=x,\\qquad L_2:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad L_3:y=-2x+5 .\n\\]\n\n- \\(L_1\\) contains \\(A,E\\).  \n- \\(L_2\\) contains \\(B,F\\).  \n- \\(L_3\\) contains \\(C,D\\).\n\nAll six points are covered and every line is sunny; hence \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \nBy the discussion in §1, the two sunny lines must be a 2‑element subset of \\(\\{L_{AE},L_{BF},L_{CD}\\}\\).\n\nWe examine each possible subset.\n\n1. **Subset \\(\\{L_{AE},L_{BF}\\}\\).**  \n   Points covered: \\(A,E\\) (by \\(L_{AE}\\)) and \\(B,F\\) (by \\(L_{BF}\\)).  \n   Uncovered points are \\(C(1,3)\\) and \\(D(2,1)\\).  \n   The unique line through \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is \\(L_{CD}\\), which is sunny.  \n   Hence the third line would be sunny, contradicting the assumption of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n2. **Subset \\(\\{L_{AE},L_{CD}\\}\\).**  \n   Covered: \\(A,E\\) and \\(C,D\\).  \n   Uncovered: \\(B(1,2)\\) and \\(F(3,1)\\).  \n   The line through \\(B\\) and \\(F\\) is \\(L_{BF}\\) (sunny), again a contradiction.\n\n3. **Subset \\(\\{L_{BF},L_{CD}\\}\\).**  \n   Covered: \\(B,F\\) and \\(C,D\\).  \n   Uncovered: \\(A(1,1)\\) and \\(E(2,2)\\).  \n   The line through \\(A\\) and \\(E\\) is \\(L_{AE}\\) (sunny), contradiction.\n\nIn every case the only line that could cover the two remaining points is itself sunny, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nTherefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll three values are attainable by the constructions of §§2.1–2.3, and the value \\(k=2\\) is ruled out by the impossibility argument of §3.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,3.}\n\\]"
