ers (1,2),(2,4),(3,6) but (3,6) sum=9 >6, not allowed. So covers (1,2),(2,4). Also (4,8) not allowed. So covers 2 points.\n\nNow we have covered (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,4). Still many points left.\n\nWe can use non-sunny diagonals: maybe use x+y=6 (covers points with sum=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)). That covers (2,4) already covered, (3,3) already covered, (5,1) new, (4,2) new, (1,5) new. So diagonal x+y=6 covers 5 points.\n\nNow we have covered: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,4),(1,5),(4,2),(5,1). Also (2,4) covered twice. Points left: (1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 7 points.\n\nWe have used 3 lines so far (y=x, y=2x, x+y=6). We need 2 more lines (both non-sunny) to cover the remaining 7 points. Non-sunny lines can be diagonals, horizontals, verticals. Let's try to use diagonal x+y=5 (covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)). That covers 4 of the remaining points. Then remaining points: (1,3),(2,1),(3,1). That's 3 points. Use diagonal x+y=4 (covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)). (2,2) already covered, (1,3) and (3,1) covered. So after using x+y=4, we have covered (1,3),(3,1). Remaining point: (2,1). Use vertical line x=2 (non-sunny) to cover (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). (2,2) and (2,4) already covered. So we have used total lines: y=x (sunny), y=2x (sunny), x+y=6, x+y=5, x+y=4, x=2. That's 6 lines, but we need exactly 5 lines. So too many.\n\nWe need to reduce number of lines. Perhaps we can use a non-sunny line that covers more of the remaining points. For example, vertical line x=1 covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Many of those are already covered except (1,3),(1,4). But we could use x=1 as a non-sunny line instead of some diagonal. Let's try a different approach.\n\nGoal: 5 lines total, 2 sunny. So we need to cover 15 points with 5 lines, average 3 points per line. That's not too high. But we need to ensure each line covers at least 3 points on average. Non-sunny lines can cover up to 5 points (diagonal x+y=6). Sunny lines can also cover multiple points. So