  \\]\n\n   Hence any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\Longleftrightarrow k(k-3)\\le0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Excluding \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   By Lemma 3 we may take the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines to be distinct diagonals, so exactly two diagonals are discarded.  \n   Let the discarded diagonals be \\(D_{s_1}\\) and \\(D_{s_2}\\) with \\(2\\le s_1<s_2\\le n+1\\).\n\n   From (1) with \\(k=2\\) we need  \n\n   \\[\n   (s_1-1)+(s_2-1)\\le4 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Set \\(a=s_1-1\\), \\(b=s_2-1\\) (\\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge a+1\\)). Equation (4) becomes \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \n   The only possibilities for distinct positive integers \\((a,b)\\) are \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\). Hence the only possible discarded sets are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{2,3\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\mathcal D=\\{2,4\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Case 1: \\(\\mathcal D=\\{2,3\\}\\).*  \n   The points involved are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,1)\\in D_2,\\qquad\n   P_2=(1,2),\\;P_3=(2,1)\\in D_3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Any two of these points determine a line whose slope is respectively vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\); all three slopes belong to the forbidden set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Consequently **no sunny line can contain two of the three points**. With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of them, leaving one uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(\\mathcal D=\\{2,4\\}\\).*  \n   The points are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_0=(1,1)\\in D_2,\\qquad\n   Q_1=(1,3),\\;Q_2=(2,2),\\;Q_3=(3,1)\\in D_4 .\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets \\(D_4\\) in at most one point, so the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points \\(Q_1,Q_2,Q_3\\). The remaining point of \\(D_4\\) would stay uncovered, because the non‑sunny lines do not contain any point of \\(D_4\\) (they are exactly the other diagonals). Hence a configuration with two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n   Both possibilities lead to contradictions; therefore **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur**. Combining with (3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Realising the remaining values (explicit verification).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in this range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_s\\). All lines have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny. Hence exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines are used.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_*:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   The only point not belonging to the retained diagonals is \\((1,1)\\) (it lies on the discarded diagonal \\(D_2\\)). Since \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\), it is covered by \\(L_*\\). Every other point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the retained diagonals, so the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered. The line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\), therefore it is sunny; exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Discard the three smallest diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-3\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The six points belonging to the discarded diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,2)\\in D_2,\\qquad\n   (1,2),(2,1)\\in D_3,\\qquad\n   (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\in D_4 .\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:\\;y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2&:\\;y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3&:\\;y = -2x + 5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification** (substituting each point):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{Point} & \\text{Line that contains it} & \\text{Check} \\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1 & 1=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1 & 2=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot2+\\tfrac52=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3 & 3=-2\\cdot1+5=3\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_3 & 1=-2\\cdot3+5=1\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore all three are sunny. Together with the \\(n-3\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines we have exactly three sunny lines and every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n   \\]"
