"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\;(2\\le s\\le n+1).\n   \\]\n   \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and \\(|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n   A line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   All other lines are **sunny**.\n\n2. **Key Lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f1.* A sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line contained two points of the same diagonal, the segment joining them would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.* A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) coincides with exactly one diagonal \\(D_c\\) and therefore contains **all** points of that diagonal; a horizontal or vertical line meets any diagonal in at most one point. \u220e  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f3 (Reduction to diagonal non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   In any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\), the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can be replaced (without increasing \\(k\\) and without losing coverage) by **distinct diagonal lines** \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line that is not a diagonal.  \n   - If \\(L\\) is vertical, \\(L:x=c\\). Its points in \\(S_n\\) are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \n     Each such point lies on a different diagonal \\(D_{c+b}\\). Choose one of those diagonals, say \\(D_{c+b_0}\\), and replace \\(L\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=c+b_0\\). That new line contains **all** points of \\(D_{c+b_0}\\), in particular the point \\((c,b_0)\\) that was on \\(L\\).  \n   - The same argument works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \n\n   Repeating the replacement for every non\u2011diagonal non\u2011sunny line yields a configuration in which all \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are distinct diagonals. \u220e  \n\n   Consequently we may assume that the set of non\u2011sunny lines is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D^{c}=\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s\\in\\mathcal S\\,\\},\n   \\qquad \n   \\mathcal S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;|\\mathcal S|=n-k .\n   \\]\n   The complementary set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\mathcal S\n   \\]\n   consists of the **discarded** diagonals; all points belonging to those diagonals must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Counting restrictions.**  \n\n   *Number of discarded diagonals.*  \n   Each discarded diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains at least one point that is not covered by the non\u2011sunny lines, therefore at least one sunny line must pass through that diagonal. By Lemma\u202f1 a single sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point, so we need **at least one distinct sunny line for each discarded diagonal**. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal D|\\le k. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Total points on discarded diagonals.*  \n   Let  \n   \\[\n   T=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal D}|D_s|=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal D}(s-1)\n   \\]\n   be the total number of points that lie on discarded diagonals.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can meet each discarded diagonal in at most one point, therefore with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\) points on each discarded diagonal and at most \\(k\\cdot|\\mathcal D|\\le k^{2}\\) points in total. Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   T\\le k^{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Necessary lower bound for \\(k\\).*  \n   For a discarded diagonal \\(D_s\\) we have \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) points, but the non\u2011sunny lines cover none of them. To cover all of them we need at least \\(s-1\\) distinct lines (sunny or non\u2011sunny). Since we already have \\(n-k\\) diagonal non\u2011sunny lines covering the other diagonals, the **additional** lines required for the discarded diagonals are at least \\((s-1)-1=s-2\\). Summing over all discarded diagonals gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal D}(s-2). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Deriving the possible values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   The right\u2011hand side of (3) is minimized when \\(\\mathcal D\\) consists of the \\(k\\) smallest indices (by (1) we may take \\(|\\mathcal D|=k\\)). Hence the minimal value is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}(s-2)=0+1+2+\\dots+(k-1)=\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\Longleftrightarrow k(k-3)\\le0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Excluding \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   By Lemma\u202f3 we may take the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be distinct diagonals, so exactly two diagonals are discarded; denote them \\(D_{s_1},D_{s_2}\\) with \\(2\\le s_1<s_2\\le n+1\\).\n\n   From (2) with \\(k=2\\) we need  \n\n   \\[\n   (s_1-1)+(s_2-1)\\le4 .\n   \\]\n\n   Setting \\(a=s_1-1\\ge1\\) and \\(b=s_2-1\\ge a+1\\) gives \\(a+b\\le4\\). The only possibilities for distinct positive integers are \\((a,b)=(1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), i.e. \\(\\{s_1,s_2\\}=\\{2,3\\}\\) or \\(\\{2,4\\}\\).\n\n   *Case \\(\\{2,3\\}\\).*  \n   The points are \\((1,1)\\) (from \\(D_2\\)) and \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) (from \\(D_3\\)). Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), all forbidden for a sunny line. Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\n   *Case \\(\\{2,4\\}\\).*  \n   The points are \\((1,1)\\) (from \\(D_2\\)) and \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (from \\(D_4\\)). By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line meets \\(D_4\\) in at most one point, so the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points of \\(D_4\\); the remaining point stays uncovered. Contradiction.\n\n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible. Combining with (4) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realising the remaining values (explicit verification).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in this range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_s\\). All lines have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny. Hence exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines are used and every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Keep the \\(n-1\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_*:\\;y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   The only diagonal not present is \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and is therefore covered by \\(L_*\\). Every other point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to one of the retained diagonals, so the whole set is covered. The line \\(y=x\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is not \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus it is sunny and exactly one sunny line appears.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Discard the three smallest diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-3\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=5,6,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The six points belonging to the discarded diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &D_2:\\;(1,1),\\\\\n   &D_3:\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\\\\n   &D_4:\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the following three sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1&:\\;y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_2&:\\;y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\n   L_3&:\\;y = -2x + 5 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   **Point\u2011by\u2011point verification**\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n   \\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} & \\text{Check}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1 & 1=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1 & 2=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_2 & 2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52=2\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_2 & 1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52=1\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_3 & 3=-2\\cdot1+5=3\\;\\checkmark\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_3 & 1=-2\\cdot2+5=1\\;\\checkmark\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore all three are sunny. Together with the \\(n-3\\) diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines we have exactly three sunny lines and every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}} .\n   \\]"
