e is **non‑sunny** if it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   All other lines are **sunny**.\n\n2. **Key Lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line contained two points of the same diagonal, the segment joining them would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 2.* A diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) coincides with exactly one diagonal \\(D_c\\) and therefore contains **all** points of that diagonal; a horizontal or vertical line meets any diagonal in at most one point. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 3 (Reduction to diagonal non‑sunny lines).*  \n   In any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\), the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can be replaced (without increasing \\(k\\) and without losing coverage) by **distinct diagonal lines** \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a non‑sunny line that is not a diagonal.  \n   - If \\(L\\) is vertical, \\(L:x=c\\). Its points in \\(S_n\\) are \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \n     Each such point lies on a different diagonal \\(D_{c+b}\\). Choose one of those diagonals, say \\(D_{c+b_0}\\), and replace \\(L\\) by the diagonal line \\(x+y=c+b_0\\). That new line contains **all** points of \\(D_{c+b_0}\\), in particular the point \\((c,b_0)\\) that was on \\(L\\).  \n   - The same argument works for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \n\n   Repeating the replacement for every non‑diagonal non‑sunny line yields a configuration in which all \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are distinct diagonals. ∎  \n\n   Consequently we may assume that the set of non‑sunny lines is  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D^{c}=\\{\\,x+y=s\\mid s\\in\\mathcal S\\,\\},\n   \\qquad \n   \\mathcal S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;|\\mathcal S|=n-k .\n   \\]\n   The complementary set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}