rigorous: there could be a configuration where some non-sunny lines are vertical/horizontal and some diagonals, but the total coverage might still be possible. But they argue that any non-sunny line can meet each diagonal at most once, so the total number of points covered by non-sunny lines is at most the sum of the sizes of the diagonals they replace. But they need to argue that using the largest diagonals is optimal. This is plausible but may need justification. However, this is a justification gap, not a critical error.\n\nNow, the main part of the impossibility for k≥4 uses the fact that each sunny line must contain exactly one point from D_{k+1} (the diagonal with sum k+1). That's correct because each sunny line can intersect D_{k+1} at most one point, and there are k points on D_{k+1} that need to be covered (since D_{k+1} is part of S_k). So each sunny line must contain exactly one of those points. So far so good.\n\nThen they consider the first column x=1. Since each sunny line is not vertical, it intersects x=1 at exactly one point. The points on the first column that need to be covered are (1,1),...,(1,k). Since there are k sunny lines, each must contain exactly one of those points. So the set of intersection points (1,y_i) must be a permutation of {1,...,k}. This is correct.\n\nNow they consider diagonal D_k (sum = k). Points Q_j = (j, k-j) for j=1,...,k-1. They argue that no L_i for i≥2 can contain any Q_j. They derive a contradiction using algebra. If that is correct, then all Q_j must be on L_1. But L_1 already contains P_1 = (1,k). Since a sunny line can intersect D_k at most one point, L_1 cannot contain all Q_j. So impossible. So the argument is sound if the algebraic derivation is correct.\n\nThus the key is whether the algebraic derivation is correct. Let's verify the derivation more carefully.\n\nWe have L_i passes through P_i = (i, k+1-i) and (1, y_i). So slope m_i = (k+1-i - y_i)/(i-1). For i≥2.\n\nEquation: y = y_i + m_i (x-1). For point Q_j = (j, k-j) to be on L_i, we need:\n\nk - j = y_i + m_i (j - 1). (Equation 3)\n\nPlug m_i:\n\nk - j = y_i + ((k+1-i - y_i)/(i-1)) (j - 1).\n\nMultiply both sides by (i-1):\n\n(k - j)(i-1) = y_i (i-1) + (k+1-i - y_i)(j - 1).\n\nExpand left: (k - j)(i-1) = (k - j)i - (k - j) = k i - k - j i + j.\n\nRight: y_i (i-1) + (k+1-i - y_i)(j - 1) = y_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1-i - y_i).\n\nSimplify right: y_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1-i - y_i) = y_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - k - 1 + i + y_i = y_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - k - 1 + i + y_i.\n\nCombine -y_i + y_i = 0. So right side simplifies to y_i i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - k - 1 + i.\n\nThus right side = i y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1) + i.\n\nNow set left = right:\n\nk i - k - j i + j = i y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1) + i.\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\nk i - k - j i + j - i y_i - (k+1-i - y_i)j + (k+1) - i = 0.\n\nGroup terms: Let's group terms with j and without j.\n\nTerms without j: k i - k - i y_i + (k+1) - i = k i - i y_i - k + k + 1 - i? Wait, we have -k + (k+1) = 1. So non-j terms: k i - i y_i + 1 - i.\n\nTerms with j: - j i - (k+1-i - y_i)j = -j i - (k+1-i - y_i)j = -j i - (k+1-i)j + y_i j = -j (i + k+1-i) + y_i j = -j (k+1) + y_i j.\n\nThus the equation becomes:\n\nk i - i y_i + 1 - i - j (k+1) + y_i j = 0.\n\nRearrange: k i - i y_i + 1 - i + j (y_i - (k+1)) = 0.\n\nThus j (y_i - (k+1)) = i y_i - k i + i - 1.\n\nMultiply both sides by -1: j ((k+1) - y_i) = k i - i y_i - i + 1.\n\nThus j (k+1 - y_i) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nNow note that k+1 - y_i = (k - y_i) + 1. So we have:\n\nj ((k - y_i) + 1) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nExpand left: j (k - y_i) + j = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nBring terms: j (k - y_i) - i (k - y_i - 1) = 1 - j.\n\nExpand i term: i (k - y_i - 1) = i (k - y_i) - i.\n\nThus left side: j (k - y_i) - i (k - y_i) + i = (j - i)(k - y_i) + i.\n\nThus (j - i)(k - y_i) + i = 1 - j.\n\nThus (j - i)(k - y_i) = 1 - j - i = -(i + j - 1).\n\nThus (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nWait, we have (j - i)(k - y_i) = -(i + j - 1). Multiply both sides by -1: (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nThus we get (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nBut earlier we derived (i - j)(k - y_i) = i - 1. There's a discrepancy. Let's re-derive carefully to avoid mistakes.\n\nLet's start again from equation (3): k - j = y_i + m_i (j - 1). With m_i = (k+1-i - y_i)/(i-1). So:\n\nk - j = y_i + ((k+1-i - y_i)/(i-1)) (j - 1).\n\nMultiply both sides by (i-1):\n\n(k - j)(i-1) = y_i (i-1) + (k+1-i - y_i)(j - 1).\n\nNow expand both sides:\n\nLeft: (k - j)(i-1) = (k - j)i - (k - j) = k i - k - j i + j.\n\nRight: y_i (i-1) = y_i i - y_i. And (k+1-i - y_i)(j - 1) = (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1-i - y_i). So right side sum:\n\ny_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1-i - y_i).\n\nSimplify the last term: -(k+1-i - y_i) = -k - 1 + i + y_i.\n\nThus right side = y_i i - y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - k - 1 + i + y_i.\n\nCombine -y_i + y_i = 0. So right side = y_i i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - k - 1 + i.\n\nThus right side = i y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1) + i.\n\nNow set left = right:\n\nk i - k - j i + j = i y_i + (k+1-i - y_i)j - (k+1) + i.\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\nk i - k - j i + j - i y_i - (k+1-i - y_i)j + (k+1) - i = 0.\n\nNow group terms:\n\nGroup terms without j: k i - k - i y_i + (k+1) - i = k i - i y_i - k + k + 1 - i = k i - i y_i + 1 - i.\n\nGroup terms with j: - j i - (k+1-i - y_i)j = -j i - (k+1-i)j + y_i j = -j (i + k+1 - i) + y_i j = -j (k+1) + y_i j.\n\nThus the equation becomes:\n\n(k i - i y_i + 1 - i) + (-j (k+1) + y_i j) = 0.\n\nThus k i - i y_i + 1 - i + j (y_i - (k+1)) = 0.\n\nThus j (y_i - (k+1)) = i y_i - k i + i - 1.\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\nj ((k+1) - y_i) = k i - i y_i - i + 1.\n\nNow note that (k+1) - y_i = (k - y_i) + 1. So:\n\nj ((k - y_i) + 1) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nExpand left: j (k - y_i) + j = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nNow bring j to right side:\n\nj (k - y_i) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1 - j.\n\nNow we can rearrange to isolate j. Let's bring terms with j to left:\n\nj (k - y_i) + j = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nBut we already had that earlier. Let's solve for j:\n\nj (k - y_i) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1 - j.\n\nThus j (k - y_i) + j = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nThus j (k - y_i + 1) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nBut we already had that earlier. Let's try to solve for j:\n\nj = [i (k - y_i - 1) + 1] / (k - y_i + 1). This is messy.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to derive a simpler relation. Let's try to bring all terms to one side:\n\nj (k - y_i) + j - i (k - y_i - 1) - 1 = 0.\n\nFactor j: j (k - y_i + 1) - i (k - y_i - 1) - 1 = 0.\n\nThus j (k - y_i + 1) = i (k - y_i - 1) + 1.\n\nNow note that k - y_i + 1 = (k - y_i) + 1, and k - y_i - 1 = (k - y_i) - 1.\n\nThus j ((k - y_i) + 1) = i ((k - y_i) - 1) + 1.\n\nNow we can try to see if this can be transformed to the solution's equation (4). Let's denote A = k - y_i. Then we have:\n\nj (A + 1) = i (A - 1) + 1.\n\nThus j (A + 1) = i A - i + 1.\n\nThus j (A + 1) = i A - i + 1.\n\nNow expand left: j A + j = i A - i + 1.\n\nBring terms: j A - i A = -i + 1 - j.\n\nThus (j - i) A = -i + 1 - j = -(i + j - 1).\n\nThus (i - j) A = i + j - 1.\n\nThus (i - j) (k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nThus we have derived (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nNow compare to the solution's equation (4): (j + i - 1)(k - y_i) = k + 1 - i - y_i.\n\nLet's see if these are equivalent. Using A = k - y_i, the RHS of solution's equation is k + 1 - i - y_i = (k - y_i) + (1 - i) = A + (1 - i). So solution's equation is (j + i - 1) A = A + (1 - i). Rearranged: (j + i - 1) A - A = 1 - i => (j + i - 2) A = 1 - i => (j + i - 2) A = -(i - 1). Multiply both sides by -1: (i - j - i + 2) A = i - 1 => (2 - j) A = i - 1. So solution's equation reduces to (2 - j) (k - y_i) = i - 1.\n\nOur derived equation is (i - j) (k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nThese are not the same. So the solution's equation (4) appears to be incorrect. However, the solution's subsequent inequality (5) uses (j + i - 1) ≤ (k+1-i-y_i)/(k-y_i). Let's see if that inequality can be derived from our correct equation. Starting from (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1, we can solve for j. Let's try to get a bound.\n\nWe have (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1.\n\nIf i - j > 0 (i > j), then left side positive, right side positive. If i ≤ j, left side ≤ 0, right side ≥? Let's examine possibilities.\n\nWe can rewrite as (i - j)(k - y_i) - (i + j - 1) = 0.\n\nAlternatively, solve for j: Expand left: (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1 => i(k - y_i) - j(k - y_i) = i + j - 1 => bring j terms together: -j(k - y_i) - j = -j[(k - y_i) + 1] = -j(k - y_i + 1). So we have i(k - y_i) = i + j - 1 + j(k - y_i) = i + j - 1 + j(k - y_i). Actually we need to bring all j terms to one side: i(k - y_i) - i = j(k - y_i) + j - 1 => i(k - y_i - 1) = j(k - y_i + 1) - 1 => j(k - y_i + 1) = i(k - y_i - 1) + 1. This matches earlier derived j (A + 1) = i (A - 1) + 1. So that's consistent.\n\nNow we can try to bound j. Since A = k - y_i > 0, we have j = [i (A - 1) + 1] / (A + 1). Since A ≥ 1 (since y_i ≤ k-1), we have A - 1 ≥ 0. So numerator i (A - 1) + 1 ≤ i A + 1. Denominator A + 1 > A. So j < (i A + 1) / (A + 1). For i ≥ 2, this is less than i (since (i A + 1) / (A + 1) < i for A ≥ 1). So j < i. So j ≤ i - 1. So j ≤ i - 1. So j + i - 1 ≤ 2i - 2. Not helpful.\n\nBut the solution's inequality (5) says (j + i - 1) ≤ (k+1-i-y_i)/(k-y_i). Since RHS < 1, they get contradiction because LHS ≥ i ≥ 2. Let's see if we can derive a similar inequality from our correct equation. Starting from (i - j) A = i + j - 1. Since A > 0, we have i - j = (i + j - 1)/A. So i - j = (i + j - 1)/A. Since A ≥ 1, we have i - j ≤ i + j - 1. That's trivial. But we can also solve for j: i - j = (i + j - 1)/A => multiply both sides by A: A(i - j) = i + j - 1 => i A - j A = i + j - 1 => bring j terms: -j A - j = -j (A + 1) = i + 1 - i A => j (A + 1) = i A - i - 1 => j = (i A - i - 1)/(A + 1). Since A = k - y_i ≤ k - 1, we have A ≤ k - 1. For i ≥ 2, numerator i A - i - 1 = i (A - 1) - 1. Since A - 1 ≥ 0 (if A ≥ 1), numerator ≥ -1. But we need integer j between 1 and k-1. Hard to get contradiction.\n\nNevertheless, the solution's algebraic derivation appears flawed. However, the conclusion that no L_i for i≥2 can contain any Q_j may still hold, but the proof given is not correct. The question is: does this constitute a \"Critical Error\" in reasoning? According to the hierarchical decision tree, Level 2 checks whether the solution uses valid mathematical principles. The solution uses case analysis, counting arguments, and algebraic manipulation. The algebraic manipulation appears to contain an error. However, does that constitute an invalid method? The method is still valid (algebraic manipulation), but the specific algebraic steps may be incorrect. According to Level 3, we classify errors in intermediate steps as \"Critical Error\" if they are demonstrably wrong and invalidate the logic chain. The solution's derivation of equation (4) and (5) is demonstrably wrong (the algebraic manipulation is incorrect). This leads to a contradiction that may not be valid. If the contradiction is not valid, the conclusion that k≥4 impossible may not be justified. So the reasoning may be invalid due to a critical error in the algebraic derivation. However, we need to decide if this is a \"Critical Error\" in the logic chain (Level 3) or a \"Justification Gap\". The error is not just a missing detail; it's a wrong intermediate step that leads to a false conclusion. That is a critical error.\n\nBut we need to see if the solution's overall reasoning can be salvaged: maybe the conclusion can be derived by a different argument, but the solution as presented contains a flawed step. According to the grading policy, we must evaluate the solution as given. If there is a demonstrably wrong intermediate step that invalidates the logic chain, we must mark it as a Critical Error at Level 3, leading to FAIL. However, we need to check if the error indeed invalidates the conclusion. The solution's claim that k≥4 impossible is based on that algebraic contradiction. If the algebraic step is wrong, the conclusion is not proven. So the reasoning is invalid. Therefore Level 2 may still be considered valid because the method (case analysis, counting, algebraic manipulation) is valid, but the specific execution of the algebraic manipulation is flawed. According to the hierarchical decision tree, Level 2 checks whether the solution uses valid mathematical principles, not whether each algebraic step is correct. However, if the algebraic manipulation is incorrect, that is a flaw in the reasoning, which may be considered a \"Critical Error\" at Level 3. The Level 2 gate is about the validity of the methods used, not the correctness of each step. The solution uses valid methods (case analysis, counting, algebraic manipulation). So Level 2 passes. Then Level 3 we examine the presentation and find a critical error in the algebraic derivation. That would be a \"Critical Error\" in the logic chain, leading to FAIL.\n\nBut we need to be careful: The hierarchical decision tree says Level 3: \"Examine the completeness and rigor of the presentation. Classify any issues found: Justification Gap (missing details, imprecise wording, incomplete verification (but logic is sound)) or Critical Error (demonstrably wrong intermediate calculations that invalidate the logic chain).\" So we need to decide if the algebraic derivation is a demonstrably wrong intermediate calculation that invalidates the logic chain. It appears to be wrong. Let's verify more concretely: The solution claims that substituting m_i into (3) yields (4): (j+i-1)(k - y_i) = k+1 - i - y_i. We derived a different equation: (i - j)(k - y_i) = i + j - 1. So the solution's equation is indeed incorrect. This is a demonstrably wrong intermediate calculation. Does this invalidates the logic chain? Yes, because the subsequent inequality (5) and contradiction rely on that equation. If the equation is wrong, the contradiction may not hold. So the conclusion that k≥4 impossible is not proven. Therefore the reasoning is invalid.\n\nThus the solution fails at Level 3 due to a critical error. According to the hierarchical decision tree, if Level 3 finds a critical error, the verdict is FAIL.\n\nBut we need to consider if there might be other ways to salvage the argument: The solution also includes a separate argument for k=2 (impossibility) which is correct. The existence for k=0,1,3 is correct. The only questionable part is the impossibility for k≥4. If that part is flawed, the answer {0,1,3} might still be correct, but the solution hasn't proven that no other k are possible. So the answer correctness is not established. However, the answer correctness is judged based on the final answer being correct. If the answer is correct, but the reasoning is flawed, we still need to see if the reasoning is valid. The answer is correct if indeed the only possible k are {0,1,3}. If the solution fails to prove that, but the answer is still correct, does that affect Level 1? Level 1 only checks if the answer is correct. The answer is correct (assuming the problem's answer is indeed {0,1,3}). So Level 1 passes. Level 2 passes because methods are valid. Level 3 fails due to critical error. So final verdict FAIL.\n\nBut we need to be sure that the answer is indeed correct. Let's try to see if there could be any other k values possible. Could k=2 be possible for some n? The solution argued it's impossible for any n≥3. That seems plausible. Could k=4 be possible for some n? The solution argued impossible for any n≥4. But we need to verify if there is any known solution for k=4. Let's think: For n=5, k=4, we have 5 lines total, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny (diagonal). The points to cover are those with a+b ≤ 6. That's S_5: points (1,1) up to (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's total points = sum_{c=2}^{6} (c-1) = (5*6)/2 = 15 points. We have 5 lines, each sunny line can cover at most 5 points (one per diagonal). So 4 sunny lines can cover at most 20 points, enough. But we need to cover all 15 points. Could we find a configuration? Possibly. But the solution claims impossible for any k≥4. Let's test for n=5, k=4. We need 4 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line (diagonal). The non-sunny line can be a diagonal x+y=c for some c. The largest diagonal is D_6 (c=6) with points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1) (5 points). So we could use that as the non-sunny line. Then we need to cover the remaining points: all points with a+b ≤5 (i.e., D_2,...,D_5). That's S_4 points (10 points). We need to cover them with 4 sunny lines. That's exactly the case n=4, k=4 we considered earlier. So if we can cover S_4 with 4 sunny lines, then we can cover S_5 with 4 sunny lines + one diagonal. So the problem reduces to covering S_4 with 4 sunny lines. So if we can find a covering for S_4 with 4 sunny lines, then k=4 would be possible for n≥4. So we need to check if S_4 can be covered by 4 sunny lines. Let's try to find a covering manually. S_4 points: (1,1), (1,2),(2,1), (1,3),(2,2),(3,1), (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). We need 4 lines, each not vertical/horizontal/diagonal slope -1. Let's try to find a covering.\n\nOne approach: Use lines y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2)). Use line y = 2x - 1 (covers (1,1),(2,3)). But (1,1) already covered, okay. Use line y = -0.5x + 2.5 (covers (1,2),(3,1)). Use line y = -2x + 5 (covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)). Let's see if these four lines cover all points:\n\n- L1: y = x covers (1,1),(2,2). Also (3,3) not needed.\n- L2: y = 2x - 1 covers (1,0) not needed, (2,3) (2,3) is needed, (3,5) not needed. It also covers (1,1) again.\n- L3: y = -0.5x + 2.5 covers (1,2) (good), (3,1) (good). Also (2,1.5) not needed.\n- L4: y = -2x + 5 covers (1,3) (good), (2,1) (good), (3,-1) not needed, (4,-3) not needed. Actually (1,3) is needed, (2,1) is needed, (3,2) is needed? Let's check: (3,2) is on y = -2*3 + 5 = -6+5 = -1, not 2. So (3,2) is not covered. (4,1) is on y = -2*4 + 5 = -8+5 = -3, not 1. So L4 does not cover (3,2) or (4,1). So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = -x + 5 (slope -1, not allowed). So we need to find a set of 4 sunny lines covering all points. Let's try systematic approach: Since each sunny line can intersect each diagonal D_c at most one point, we need to cover each diagonal D_2 (1 point), D_3 (2 points), D_4 (3 points), D_5 (4 points). With 4 lines, each line can intersect each diagonal at most one point, so total capacity per diagonal is at most 4 points (since we have 4 lines). D_5 has 4 points, so we need each of the 4 lines to intersect D_5 at a distinct point. So each line must contain exactly one point from D_5. Similarly, D_4 has 3 points, so at most 3 of the lines intersect D_4 at a point; one line must intersect D_4 at no point. D_3 has 2 points, at most 2 lines intersect D_3. D_2 has 1 point, at most 1 line intersects D_2. So we need to assign points to lines such that each line gets at most one point per diagonal, and each point is covered. This is like a Latin rectangle problem. Let's try to assign:\n\nPoints on D_5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Let's assign each to a distinct line L1-L4.\n\nPoints on D_4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). We need to assign each to a line, but we cannot assign a point to a line that already has a point on D_5 that would cause the line to have two points on different diagonals, which is allowed. So we can assign each D_4 point to a line, possibly the same line as a D_5 point, as long as the line passes through both points. But a line can intersect D_5 at one point and D_4 at one point, that's okay. So we need to find lines that pass through a point from D_5 and a point from D_4, etc.\n\nWe need to find 4 lines that each pass through one point from D_5, and collectively cover all points from D_4, D_3, D_2. This is a combinatorial design problem. Let's try to find a solution manually.\n\nWe can try to pair points: For each line, we can choose a point from D_5 and maybe also a point from D_4, D_3, D_2. Let's try to find lines:\n\n- Choose line L1 through (1,4) and (2,2). Slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2. So L1: y = -2x + 6. Check: (1,4) ok, (2,2) ok. Does it also pass through any other required points? (3,0) not needed. It also passes through (3,0) not needed. So L1 covers (1,4) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- Choose line L2 through (2,3) and (3,1). Slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2. So L2: y = -2x + 7? Let's compute: Using point (2,3): y - 3 = -2(x - 2) => y = -2x + 7. Check (3,1): y = -2*3 + 7 = 1, ok. So L2: y = -2x + 7. It also passes through (1,5) not needed. So L2 covers (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\n- Choose line L3 through (3,2) and (1,2). Slope = (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't use that. Maybe choose (3,2) and (2,1). Slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = (-1)/(-1) = 1. So line y = x - 1? Let's compute: Using (2,1): y - 1 = 1*(x - 2) => y = x - 1. Check (3,2): y = 3 - 1 = 2, ok. So L3: y = x - 1. This line is sunny (slope 1). It also passes through (1,0) not needed. So L3 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\n- Choose line L4 through (4,1) and (1,1). Slope = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So can't use that. Maybe (4,1) and (2,2)? Slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -0.5. So line through (4,1) and (2,2): slope = -0.5. Equation: y - 2 = -0.5 (x - 2) => y = -0.5x + 3. So L4: y = -0.5x + 3. Check (4,1): y = -0.5*4 + 3 = -2 + 3 = 1, ok. So L4 covers (4,1) and (2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L1. That's okay, but we need to cover (1,3) and (1,2) and (1,1) maybe. Let's see which points remain uncovered after L1-L4:\n\nPoints covered so far:\n- L1: (1,4), (2,2)\n- L2: (2,3), (3,1)\n- L3: (2,1), (3,2)\n- L4: (4,1), (2,2) (duplicate)\n\nRemaining points: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (3,1) already covered, (1,4) covered, (2,1) covered, (2,2) covered, (2,3) covered, (3,2) covered, (4,1) covered. So left: (1,1), (1,2), (1,3). Also (3,1) is covered. So we need to cover (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). We have used all 4 lines already. So we need to adjust lines to also cover those points. Perhaps we can modify L4 to pass through (1,1) instead of (2,2). Let's try to find a line that cove