-1)(k+1) - (j-1)i - (i-1)k + (i-1)j. Simplify: (j-1)(k+1) = (j-1)k + (j-1). So RHS = (j-1)k + (j-1) - (j-1)i - (i-1)k + (i-1)j = combine k terms: (j-1)k - (i-1)k = (j-i)k. Combine constant terms: (j-1) - (j-1)i + (i-1)j = (j-1) - (j-1)i + (i-1)j. Expand (i-1)j = ij - j. So sum: (j-1) - (j-1)i + ij - j = (j-1) - (j-1)i + ij - j = combine j terms: (j-1) - j = -1. So we have -1 - (j-1)i + ij = -1 - ij + i + ij = -1 + i. Wait, check: (j-1)i = ij - i. So - (j-1)i = -ij + i. So -1 - ij + i + ij = -1 + i. So RHS = (j-i)k + (i-1). So RHS = k(j-i) + (i-1). So we have y_i (j-i) = k(j-i) + (i-1). Multiply both sides by -1: y_i (i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). Wait, we have y_i (j-i) = k(j-i) + (i-1). Multiply both sides by -1: y_i (i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). But the solution's (8) says y_i(i-j) = k(i-1-j) + (k+1-i). Let's see if these are equivalent. Let's compute k(i-1-j) + (k+1-i) = k(i-1-j) + k+1-i = k(i-1-j) + k + 1 - i = k(i-1-j+1) + 1 - i = k(i - j) + 1 - i. So RHS = k(i-j) + (1 - i). That's k(i-j) - (i-1). So indeed y_i(i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). That's exactly what we derived: y_i(i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). So the solution's (8) is consistent: y_i(i-j) = k(i-1-j) + (k+1-i) = k(i-j) - (i-1). Good.\n\nThen they divide by (i-j) (non-zero) to get y_i = k + (k+1-i)/(i-j). Wait, from y_i(i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). So y_i = k - (i-1)/(i-j). But they have y_i = k + (k+1-i)/(i-j). Let's check: Starting from y_i(i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). So y_i = k - (i-1)/(i-j). But they have y_i = k + (k+1-i)/(i-j). Are these equivalent? Let's compute (k+1-i)/(i-j) = -(k+1-i)/(j-i). Meanwhile (i-1)/(i-j) = (i-1)/(i-j). Not obviously same. Let's see if we made a sign error. Let's re-derive from (7) more carefully.\n\nEquation (7): (i-1)(k-j-y_i) = (k+1-i-y_i)(j-1). Expand both sides:\n\nLeft: (i-1)(k-j-y_i) = (i-1)(k-j) - (i-1) y_i.\n\nRight: (k+1-i-y_i)(j-1) = (j-1)(k+1-i) - (j-1) y_i.\n\nSet them equal:\n\n(i-1)(k-j) - (i-1) y_i = (j-1)(k+1-i) - (j-1) y_i.\n\nBring y_i terms to left side: -(i-1) y_i + (j-1) y_i = (j-1)(k+1-i) - (i-1)(k-j).\n\nThus y_i (-(i-1) + (j-1)) = (j-1)(k+1-i) - (i-1)(k-j).\n\nSimplify left: y_i (j-i). So y_i (j-i) = (j-1)(k+1-i) - (i-1)(k-j). Good.\n\nNow compute RHS: (j-1)(k+1-i) = (j-1)(k+1) - (j-1)i = (j-1)k + (j-1) - (j-1)i.\n\n(i-1)(k-j) = (i-1)k - (i-1)j.\n\nThus RHS = (j-1)k + (j-1) - (j-1)i - [(i-1)k - (i-1)j] = (j-1)k + (j-1) - (j-1)i - (i-1)k + (i-1)j.\n\nCombine k terms: (j-1)k - (i-1)k = (j-i)k.\n\nCombine constant terms: (j-1) - (j-1)i + (i-1)j = (j-1) - (j-1)i + ij - j = (j-1) - (j-1)i + ij - j = combine j terms: (j-1) - j = -1. So we have -1 - (j-1)i + ij = -1 - (ij - i) + ij = -1 - ij + i + ij = -1 + i. So constant part = i - 1.\n\nThus RHS = (j-i)k + (i-1). So we have y_i (j-i) = (j-i)k + (i-1). Multiply both sides by -1: y_i (i-j) = (i-j)k - (i-1). So y_i (i-j) = k(i-j) - (i-1). So y_i = k - (i-1)/(i-j). That's the derived expression.\n\nNow the solution's (9) says y_i = k + (k+1-i)/(i-j). Let's see if these are equivalent. Starting from y_i = k - (i-1)/(i-j). Write (i-1) = (i - (k+1) + (k+1) - 1)?? Not helpful. Let's try to see if we can rewrite (i-1)/(i-j) as -(k+1-i)/(i-j) + something? Actually (i-1) = (i - (k+1) + (k+1) - 1) = (i - (k+1)) + (k). So (i-1) = (i - (k+1)) + k. Then (i-1)/(i-j) = (i - (k+1))/(i-j) + k/(i-j). So y_i = k - [(i - (k+1))/(i-j) + k/(i-j)] = k - (i - (k+1))/(i-j) - k/(i-j) = k - (i - (k+1))/(i-j) - k/(i-j). Combine k terms: k - k/(i-j) = k * (1 - 1/(i-j)). Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution made a mistake in algebra. Let's check the solution's derivation: They had (8) as y_i(i-j) = k(i-1-j) + (k+1-i). Let's compute that: k(i-1-j) + (k+1-i) = k(i - 1 - j) + (k+1-i) = k(i - j - 1) + k + 1 - i = k(i - j - 1) + k + 1 - i = k(i - j - 1 + 1) + 1 - i = k(i - j) + 1 - i = k(i - j) - (i - 1). So indeed (8) is equivalent to y_i(i-j) = k(i - j) - (i - 1). So (8) matches our derived expression. Good.\n\nNow they divide by (i-j) to get y_i = k + (k+1-i)/(i-j). Let's see if that matches: Starting from y_i(i-j) =