e have:\n\n- L1: y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2))\n- L2: y = x + 1 (covers (1,2),(2,3))\n- L3: y = -2x + 5 (covers (1,3),(2,1))\n- L4: y = -2x + 6 (covers (1,4),(2,2))\n\nNow covered points:\n\nL1: (1,1),(2,2)\nL2: (1,2),(2,3)\nL3: (1,3),(2,1)\nL4: (1,4),(2,2) (duplicate)\n\nRemaining points: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1). Also (3,2) is covered? Not yet. (3,2) not covered. (3,1) not covered. (4,1) not covered. Also (3,? maybe (3,? ) we have (3,1) from D_4, (3,2) from D_5, (4,1) from D_5. So we still have 3 points uncovered.\n\nWe have used all 4 lines. So we need to adjust lines to cover those points.\n\nMaybe we can use line y = -0.5x + 2.5 (covers (1,2),(3,1)). But (1,2) already covered by L2. Could replace L2 with something else.\n\nLet's try a different set:\n\nGoal: cover points (1,1),(2,2) maybe with L1: y=x.\n\nCover (1,2),(3,1) with L2: y = -0.5x + 2.5.\n\nCover (1,3),(2,1) with L3: y = -2x + 5.\n\nCover (1,4),(2,3) with L4: y = -x + 5? Wait slope -1 is forbidden. So not that. Maybe L4: y = -2x + 6 covers (1,4),(2,2). But (2,2) already covered. Could we instead cover (2,3) and (4,1) with a line? Let's see slope between (2,3) and (4,1) = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1 (forbidden). So not allowed. (2,3) and (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1 (forbidden). (2,3) and (1,4) slope = (4-3)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1 (forbidden). So any line through (2,3) and any other point in D_5 will have slope -1? Let's check (2,3) and (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, yes. (2,3) and (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, yes. (2,3) and (1,4): slope = (4-3)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1. So any line through (2,3) and any other point in D_5 is slope -1, which is forbidden. So (2,3) cannot be paired with any other point from D_5 on a sunny line. It can be paired with points from other diagonals, but not with other D_5 points. So (2,3) must be on a line that includes points from other diagonals, not from D_5. Similarly, (1,4) cannot be paired with any other D_5 point (since only D_5 points are (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)). Let's ch