t passes through any point already covered, e.g., y=x (covers (1,1) again). That's okay. So we have a configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines: one sunny line covering (2,1) (maybe also other points, but we can choose a line that passes through (2,1) and some other point not already covered, but must be sunny). For example, line through (2,1) and (3,2) is slope 1, sunny. (3,2) is already covered by diagonal D_5, but that's fine. So we can have sunny line L1: y = x - 1 (covers (2,1),(3,2),(4,3),...). That's sunny. The second sunny line can be any sunny line, e.g., y = x (covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),...). That's also sunny. So we have exactly 2 sunny lines, n-3 diagonal lines, and one vertical line. That yields total n lines. Does this configuration satisfy the condition that exactly k=2 lines are sunny? Yes. Are all lines distinct? Yes: vertical line x=1, diagonal lines D_4,...,D_{n+1}, sunny lines y=x-1 and y=x. Are any of these lines parallel to each other? The diagonal lines are all slope -1, vertical line is slope ∞, sunny lines have slopes 1 and 1 (both slope 1). Wait, y=x and y=x-1 are parallel (both slope 1). Are they allowed to be distinct lines? Yes, they are distinct lines (different intercepts). The condition only says lines must be distinct; they can be parallel. So that's fine.\n\nThus we have a construction for any n≥3 with k=2? Let's check for n=3: n-3 = 0, so we have no diagonal lines D_4,...,D_{n+1} because n+1=4, D_4 is the only diagonal beyond D_3. Actually for n=3, D_4 exists (c=4). n-3 = 0, so we have 0 diagonal lines? Wait, we need n-3 diagonal lines, but n-3 = 0, so we have none. But we need to cover D_4 points (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). We have vertical line x=1 covering (1,3). We have two sunny lines: y=x-1 (covers (2,1) and (3,2) but (3,2) is not in our set because a+b=5 > n+1=4). Actually for n=3, the set of points is only up to a+b ≤ 4. So (3,2) is not in the set. So y=x-1 covers (2,1) only. y=x covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). (3,3) is not in the set becau